---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Work · San Lee, Technical Program Manager"
  description="A complete index of San Lee's AI systems, evaluation, technical program management, reliability, and infrastructure projects."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/work.html"
  ogType="website"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* ORIGIN: src/pages/index.astro, style island, at 6859243. Both blocks
       below moved here with the units they style, when the eight-row decision
       ledger and the field-note clusters moved off the homepage (landing
       WO-E1.1). The declarations are the homepage's, unchanged.

       ONE selector changed, and only the ix-s1 one: `.ledger li` became
       `.measure .ledger li`. This page already carries two other ul.ledger
       lists, and those rows are a link followed by a sentence rather than a
       .led-claim / .led-to pair. Unscoped, the 48rem grid squeezes each of
       those descriptions into a 10.5rem cell. `.measure` is the
       containment the rule always relied on: on the homepage this block only
       ever sat inside section.measure, so the scope states what was already
       true rather than adding a condition. */

    /* ix-s1. The decision ledger, re-laid as a strip. Eight rows carry one
       grammar: the claim, then the page that records it in full. The strip
       re-lays the row. It does not rewrite it. The STE conversion of the same
       rows is a separate edit in the same commit, and the pull request keeps
       the two ledger blocks apart.

       The strip adds no status word. Three rows carry data-outcome="reversal"
       and five do not, and the five are not five shipped decisions: one is an
       audit finding and one is a bug found before publication. A status stamp
       on the unmarked five would add a claim the page never made. The only
       mark here is the reversal mark the row already carried.

       The link stays inline in the claim below 48rem, and becomes its own
       cell only above it. scripts/hit-target.cjs measures a standalone anchor
       at 390px, and a link that is the only text of its block owes a 44px box
       there. Eight of those boxes cost the phone reader about 0.2 screens and
       return no reading gain, so the phone keeps the WCAG 2.5.8 inline
       exception that this row already relies on. */
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .measure .ledger li {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 10.5rem;
        gap: 4px 24px;
        align-items: start;
      }
      .measure .ledger li .led-to {
        justify-self: end;
        text-align: right;
      }
    }

    /* ix-s2. Twelve field notes under five cluster headings. Every one of the
       twelve titles is byte-exact. The five headings are the only new words,
       and each one is drawn from the vocabulary the titles already use. A
       heading is a structural label. It states no result.

       The label reuses .kicker, which is the site's own label grammar, so this
       block adds no second idiom, no new colour, and no new size. */
    .notes-clusters {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(210px, 1fr));
      gap: 0 32px;
      margin: 16px 0 10px;
    }
    .notes-clusters .notes-cluster { min-width: 0; }
    .notes-clusters .kicker { margin: 16px 0 0; }
    .notes-clusters .notes-list {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      margin: 6px 0 0;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <p class="kicker">Complete index</p>
    <h1>Work</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">The homepage is curated. Nothing is hidden here.</p>

    <h2>Flagship case studies</h2>
    <ul class="doors-index" aria-label="Flagship case studies">
      <li><a href="projects/telltale.html"><span class="door-name">telltale</span><span class="door-why">Five agent CLIs on one brief, and gauges that render absent rather than zero</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="projects/the-system.html"><span class="door-name">The System</span><span class="door-why">Architecture, interfaces, asynchronous delivery, and a contract seam I found broken and closed</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html"><span class="door-name">Defense News Classifier</span><span class="door-why">Human-labeled evaluation, reversals, and evidence-gated model decisions</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html"><span class="door-name">Faithfulness Judge</span><span class="door-why">Whether an LLM judge can reliably identify unsupported claims</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="projects/product-and-program.html"><span class="door-name">Product &amp; Program</span><span class="door-why">Roadmap, dependencies, risks, metrics, and the limits of a solo simulation</span></a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Evaluation and reliability</h2>
    <ul class="ledger">
      <li><a href="projects/classifier-baseline.html#try-it">Classifier baseline and live demo</a> &mdash; run the TF-IDF logistic-regression baseline in the browser.</li>
      <li><a href="projects/false-green.html">False Green</a> &mdash; six checks that passed when the required work never ran.</li>
      <li><a href="projects/kb-agent-retrieval.html">Retrieval, Measured</a> &mdash; a 27-query gold set and three paired retrieval experiments.</li>
      <li><a href="projects/autonomy-ladder.html">The Autonomy Ladder</a> &mdash; four levels of agent autonomy, with two shipped and two rejected.</li>
      <li><a href="projects/loop-replay.html">Loop Replay</a> &mdash; interactive playback of two autonomous improvement runs.</li>
      <li><a href="projects/context-architecture.html">Context Architecture</a> &mdash; an audit of relevance, sufficiency, isolation, economy, and provenance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Systems and infrastructure</h2>
    <ul class="ledger">
      <li><a href="projects/the-system-run.html">One Note, End to End</a> &mdash; one payload traced across the real service contracts.</li>
      <li><a href="projects/netops-lab.html">Zero-Touch Provisioning</a> &mdash; a factory-blank router configured after one power cycle.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- The eight one-row decisions, moved whole from index.astro at 6859243
         (landing WO-E1.1). Every row, its id, its reversal mark, its sr-only
         prefix, and its ix-s1 CSS travelled byte-exact; the CSS sits in this
         page's head with its origin note. The intro line lost one word: it
         read "Eight more, one row each" on the homepage, where the three full
         decision cards above it were the thing there were eight more of. That
         referent does not exist here, so the positional word is gone and the
         count, the shape, and the link promise are unchanged.

         section.measure is the containment this block had on the homepage. It
         re-establishes the page grid, so the rows sit on the same left edge
         as the prose above them, and it is what scopes the ix-s1 grid off
         this page's other two ledger lists. -->
    <section class="measure">
      <h2 id="decision-log">The decision log</h2>
      <p class="ledger-intro">Eight decisions, one row each. Each row links to the page that records it in full.</p>
      <!-- ix-s1, the verdict strip. Each row holds the claim and then the page
           that records it. The reversal mark and the sr-only prefix stay on the
           three rows that already carried them, and no row gains a status word.
           Five rows are not five shipped decisions: decision-falsegreen is an
           audit finding and decision-truncation is a bug found before
           publication. The CSS note in the head records why the link only
           leaves the sentence above 48rem. -->
      <ul class="ledger">
        <li id="decision-contracts"><span class="led-claim">I decoupled the repos with frozen HTTP contracts. Then I audited the seam and found it <strong>broken with both repos' CI green</strong>. Each side asserted against its own copy of the shape. Two unit tests that happen to agree are not a contract test.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/the-system.html">The System &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-falsegreen"><span class="led-claim">I audited my own gates. Six of them reported success for work that never ran. One review lane went green in <strong>36 seconds</strong> and posted nothing. The counter I built to find that failure read a key that does not exist.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/false-green.html">False Green &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-hybrid" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="led-claim"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>I built hybrid lexical retrieval to rescue one specific miss. I measured it and kept the dense-only default. The measurement returned <strong>MRR identical to four decimals</strong>. The miss it was built for was not a lexical failure at all.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/kb-agent-retrieval.html">Retrieval &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-truncation"><span class="led-claim">I nearly published a dramatic premium-vs-cheap model gap. The gap was <strong>my own bug</strong>. A <code>max_tokens</code> cap truncated the cheap tier before its verdict on 20% of claims. I counted each one as a disagreement.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html">Judge &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-ceiling"><span class="led-claim">I called it a ceiling. Then I looked anyway. The misses clustered on one boundary. Two rubric clauses moved category <strong>90.7% &rarr; 94.4%</strong>, and nothing regressed. It was a prompt problem, not a ceiling.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-prompt" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="led-claim"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>A prompt read better to a human and measured worse. Category moved <strong>79.0% &rarr; 76.7%</strong>, and industry recall moved 0.217 &rarr; 0.100. I reversed it.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-rightsized" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="led-claim"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>I built the enrichment loop on Kafka to learn it properly. Then I cut it. A single-user idempotent workload <strong>never needed a distributed log</strong>. It is now an in-process background task.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/the-system.html">The System &rarr;</a></span></li>
        <li id="decision-tooluse"><span class="led-claim">I forced output to a tool schema instead of a prose parse. The API layer rejects an invalid label. It rejected <strong>one out-of-enum response in 300</strong>, and a re-sample handled it.</span> <span class="led-to"><a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- The field-note section, moved whole from index.astro at 6859243
         (landing WO-E1.1). The twelve titles, the five cluster headings, the
         two framing sentences, and the ix-s2 CSS travelled byte-exact. The
         heading takes this page's own h2 grammar and gains the anchor id; its
         text does not change. -->
    <section class="measure">
      <h2 id="field-notes">Field notes</h2>
      <p>I keep these notes as I learn. They are in plain language, on the techniques behind all of this. <a href="https://sanlee-ys.github.io/learning-notes/">Read them &rarr;</a></p>
      <!-- ix-s2, the note clusters. The twelve titles are byte-exact and the
           order is the only thing that moved. The five headings are structural
           labels and each one is drawn from the words the titles already use.
           A heading names a subject. It states no result and it adds no
           assertion the notes site does not already carry. -->
      <div class="notes-clusters">
        <div class="notes-cluster">
          <p class="kicker">Tool use</p>
          <ul class="notes-list">
            <li>Structured output via tool-use</li>
            <li>Tool use, the general idea</li>
            <li>The agentic tool-use loop</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="notes-cluster">
          <p class="kicker">Eval</p>
          <ul class="notes-list">
            <li>Eval-driven development</li>
            <li>Reading the numbers</li>
            <li>Synthetic data and circular eval</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="notes-cluster">
          <p class="kicker">RAG</p>
          <ul class="notes-list">
            <li>RAG: answering from your own docs</li>
            <li>Embeddings and vector stores</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="notes-cluster">
          <p class="kicker">Model routing</p>
          <ul class="notes-list">
            <li>Tiered model routing</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="notes-cluster">
          <p class="kicker">Environments and checkpoints</p>
          <ul class="notes-list">
            <li>Secrets and env</li>
            <li>Reproducible environments with uv</li>
            <li>Checkpoint, resume, and retries</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted-line">This is a selection. The notes site carries the full set, and the set still grows.</p>
    </section>

    <p>Looking for terminology used across the projects? <a href="glossary.html">Open the glossary &rarr;</a></p>
